---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../consts';

// Synseis currently has almost no organic search footprint. This page is the
// cheapest way to fix that: an indexed, entity-linked description of the
// company that points at synseis.com.
const orgSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Organization',
  name: 'Synseis',
  url: 'https://synseis.com',
  description:
    'AI-native custom silicon design. Agentic, FPGA-first tooling that compresses the path from specification to GDSII.',
  founder: { '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#person` },
  areaServed: 'Worldwide',
  knowsAbout: [
    'Electronic design automation',
    'RTL generation',
    'FPGA prototyping',
    'Hardware accelerators',
    'Agentic chip design',
  ],
};
---

<Base
  title="Synseis — AI-native chip design | Neel R. Vora, founding team"
  description="Synseis builds agentic, FPGA-first tooling for custom silicon design, compressing spec-to-GDSII from months to weeks. Neel R. Vora leads hardware research on the founding team."
  schema={orgSchema}
>
  <div class="shell hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">Founding team · Hardware research</span>
    <h1>Synseis</h1>
    <p class="thesis">
      Custom silicon takes eighteen to twenty-four months and a team that
      already knows the tools. Synseis is an attempt to make that a matter of
      weeks, and to make it approachable by people who are not career EDA
      engineers.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <h2>The product</h2>
      <p>
        Synseis Labs is an early-access desktop application — macOS and Linux —
        that runs an agentic design flow: an accelerator specification goes in,
        and architecture, RTL, an FPGA project, synthesis and implementation
        come out, with hardware-in-the-loop validation rather than simulation
        alone.
      </p>

      <h2>Why this, and why now</h2>
      <p>
        Most AI-for-hardware tools are assistants. They help an engineer who
        already has the flow set up and the licences paid for. That leaves the
        two hardest barriers untouched: vendor lock-in, and the fact that
        getting started at all requires expertise most teams do not have.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Vendor-agnostic rather than tied to one toolchain</li>
        <li>Hardware-in-the-loop verification, not simulation only</li>
        <li>A reusable design corpus that compounds across projects</li>
        <li>Built so a team of five can attempt a full-scale design</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where it stands</h2>
      <p>
        Early access, with a university pilot program running — student users
        and academic designs in development.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <a href="https://synseis.com">synseis.com</a> — for access, pilots, or
        investor conversations.
      </div>

      <h2>My role</h2>
      <p>
        I cover hardware research on the founding team: accelerator
        architecture, the RTL generation path, and the question of what an
        agent actually needs to know about a target device to produce a design
        that closes timing on it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
